.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt ICOWM-RULES 5
.Os icowm-1.0.1
.Sh NAME
.Nm rules.json
.Nd per-window rules for IcoWM
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa rules.json
matches windows as they appear and applies settings to the ones that
match: which desktop a browser opens on, which windows never take focus,
which are pinned to every desktop.
The file is optional, and
.Xr icowm 1
carries on without any rules when it is absent or unparseable.
.Pp
The file is either an array of rule objects, or an object with a
.Ic rules
array.
Rules are evaluated in the order written, and where several match the
same window, a later one overrides an earlier one field by field, not
wholesale.
.Pp
A rule has three fields:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic when Pq string, default Ql \&"map\&"
When the rule may run.
.Ql map
decides once, as the window first appears.
.Ql property
runs only after a later change to a tracked property such as the title,
the role or the type.
.Ql both
does each.
.It Ic match Pq object
What the window must look like.
Omitted or empty, the rule matches every window.
.It Ic apply Pq object
What to do about it.
Absent, the rule is ignored.
.El
.Ss Matching
Every field of
.Ic match
takes either one string or an array of them, in which case any one
matching is enough.
All the fields given must match together.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic match.instance
.It Ic match.class
The two halves of
.Ql WM_CLASS .
.It Ic match.role
.Ql WM_WINDOW_ROLE .
.It Ic match.title
The window title.
.It Ic match.type
The EWMH window type, such as
.Ql dialog
or
.Ql dock .
.It Ic match.transient Pq boolean
Whether the window is transient for another.
.El
.Ss Applying
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic apply.desktop Pq integer
Send the window to this desktop.
.It Ic apply.monitor Pq integer
Place it on this monitor.
.It Ic apply.layer Pq string
Put it in this stacking layer.
.It Ic apply.focus Pq boolean
Whether it takes focus on appearing.
.It Ic apply.pinned Pq boolean
Whether it shows on every desktop.
.It Ic apply.decorated Pq boolean
Whether it gets a frame and titlebar.
.It Ic apply.opacity Pq integer or object
Desired opacity, 0 to 100, overriding the theme's own for this one
window.
One value serves both states, or an object with
.Ic active
and
.Ic inactive
sets each; an object naming only one of the two leaves the other
following the theme.
.Pp
This is published on the window through
.Ql _NET_WM_WINDOW_OPACITY
and nothing more.
.Xr icowm 1
never composites anything itself, so the property has no visible effect
whatever unless a compositing manager, such as
.Xr picom 1 ,
is also running and reading it back.
Without one, setting this changes nothing on screen.
.It Ic apply.position
Where to put the window, overriding the placement policy.
Either an object with
.Ic x
and
.Ic y ,
an absolute pixel position, or the string
.Ql center ,
which centers the window at the moment the rule runs rather than
pinning it to a fixed point.
.Pp
Combining
.Ql center
with an explicit
.Ic size
centers the window at that new size, not at whatever size it happened
to have already been given.
.Pp
When
.Ic monitor
is set as well, every position here becomes relative to that monitor's
own top left corner rather than to the whole surface, and
.Ql center
targets that monitor.
A rule naming a
.Ic monitor
and no
.Ic position
at all centers on it.
.It Ic apply.size.width Pq integer
.It Ic apply.size.height Pq integer
Give it this size.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME}/icowm/rules.json
The file this page describes, at the first of the several locations
.Xr icowm 1
looks in.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Put every terminal on desktop 1 and give it focus, and keep a music
player pinned and undecorated wherever it goes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    "rules": [
        {
            "when": "map",
            "match": { "class": "XTerm" },
            "apply": { "desktop": 1, "focus": true }
        },
        {
            "when": "both",
            "match": { "class": ["Cmus", "Ncmpcpp"] },
            "apply": { "pinned": true, "decorated": false }
        }
    ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
Dim every window that is not focused, which needs a compositing manager
running to be seen at all, and never let a notification take focus
away:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    "rules": [
        {
            "match": {},
            "apply": { "opacity": { "active": 100, "inactive": 85 } }
        },
        {
            "match": { "type": "notification" },
            "apply": { "focus": false }
        }
    ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
Give a calculator a fixed size and center it on the second monitor,
which is where the size being resolved first matters:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    "rules": [
        {
            "match": { "class": "Xcalc" },
            "apply": {
                "monitor": 1,
                "position": "center",
                "size": { "width": 400, "height": 500 }
            }
        }
    ]
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
